1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,070
It has started.

2
00:00:02,280 --> 00:00:05,200
At Anthropic, one of the biggest AI labs,

3
00:00:05,240 --> 00:00:06,360
more than eighty percent

4
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:08,640
of the code going into its codebase

5
00:00:08,680 --> 00:00:11,360
is now written by its own AI.

6
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:12,960
Some of it is code for the next

7
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:14,657
version of that AI.

8
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:17,680
For the first time in human history,

9
00:00:17,720 --> 00:00:19,200
a tool is helping to make

10
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:20,817
its own successor.

11
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:22,880
In twenty twenty-one,

12
00:00:22,920 --> 00:00:24,320
Daniel Kokotajlo,

13
00:00:24,360 --> 00:00:25,920
then a young researcher,

14
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:27,640
wrote down what he thought the world

15
00:00:27,680 --> 00:00:30,240
would look like in twenty twenty-six.

16
00:00:30,280 --> 00:00:32,000
Much of it came true.

17
00:00:32,040 --> 00:00:35,080
In twenty twenty-four he quit OpenAI,

18
00:00:35,120 --> 00:00:36,400
saying he'd lost trust it

19
00:00:36,440 --> 00:00:38,640
would handle this responsibly.

20
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:41,080
Then he refused to sign a gag agreement,

21
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:42,560
with about two million dollars

22
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:44,560
of his equity at stake.

23
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:46,240
Then he and four colleagues

24
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:48,240
wrote out the next few years,

25
00:00:48,280 --> 00:00:49,480
month by month,

26
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:51,040
with two endings.

27
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:52,640
One goes badly.

28
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:54,390
One goes a lot better.

29
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:55,600
Which one we're heading

30
00:00:55,640 --> 00:00:58,240
for comes down to a few choices.

31
00:00:58,280 --> 00:01:00,550
Some of them got made this summer.

32
00:01:01,359 --> 00:01:03,639
Let's start with what he got right.

33
00:01:03,679 --> 00:01:05,399
When someone went back and graded

34
00:01:05,439 --> 00:01:07,639
the first three years of his story,

35
00:01:07,679 --> 00:01:10,079
more than half of it had come true.

36
00:01:10,119 --> 00:01:12,199
Anthropic says its engineers merge

37
00:01:12,239 --> 00:01:14,759
about eight times as much code per day

38
00:01:14,799 --> 00:01:17,079
as they did in twenty twenty-four.

39
00:01:17,119 --> 00:01:20,319
OpenAI says its AI agents put in about

40
00:01:20,359 --> 00:01:22,599
three days of research work for every

41
00:01:22,639 --> 00:01:24,999
day its human researchers do.

42
00:01:25,039 --> 00:01:27,399
METR, an independent lab,

43
00:01:27,439 --> 00:01:29,439
measures how long a task an AI

44
00:01:29,479 --> 00:01:31,799
can finish about half the time.

45
00:01:31,839 --> 00:01:33,799
Long as in, how long it

46
00:01:33,839 --> 00:01:35,799
would take a skilled person.

47
00:01:35,839 --> 00:01:37,079
In twenty nineteen,

48
00:01:37,119 --> 00:01:38,759
that was a few seconds.

49
00:01:38,799 --> 00:01:41,239
Last year, around two hours.

50
00:01:41,279 --> 00:01:44,189
This spring, at least sixteen hours.

51
00:01:44,399 --> 00:01:46,829
That's as far as their tests go.

52
00:01:48,159 --> 00:01:49,479
Full disclosure:

53
00:01:49,519 --> 00:01:51,999
we make this channel with help from AI,

54
00:01:52,039 --> 00:01:53,309
Claude included.

55
00:01:53,439 --> 00:01:54,679
And most of these numbers

56
00:01:54,719 --> 00:01:55,959
come from the companies,

57
00:01:55,999 --> 00:01:57,309
about themselves.

58
00:01:57,599 --> 00:01:59,469
So, an outside check.

59
00:01:59,599 --> 00:02:01,479
In a small study last year,

60
00:02:01,519 --> 00:02:02,919
experienced programmers

61
00:02:02,959 --> 00:02:05,149
using AI felt faster.

62
00:02:05,359 --> 00:02:08,279
Timed, they were nineteen percent slower.

63
00:02:08,319 --> 00:02:10,599
Even Anthropic says AI improving

64
00:02:10,639 --> 00:02:12,829
itself "is not inevitable."

65
00:02:13,359 --> 00:02:14,999
Where does that leave us?

66
00:02:15,039 --> 00:02:16,999
I'd say the takeoff has started,

67
00:02:17,039 --> 00:02:18,919
but only the first stage.

68
00:02:18,959 --> 00:02:20,829
People still check the work.

69
00:02:20,879 --> 00:02:22,839
And the brakes still work.

70
00:02:22,879 --> 00:02:24,349
Hold on to that.

71
00:02:25,118 --> 00:02:27,548
This moment is shorter than it feels.

72
00:02:27,838 --> 00:02:30,008
Squeeze the whole human story,

73
00:02:30,118 --> 00:02:32,258
all three hundred thousand years,

74
00:02:32,298 --> 00:02:33,528
into one day.

75
00:02:33,898 --> 00:02:35,458
Writing shows up in the last

76
00:02:35,498 --> 00:02:36,808
twenty-four minutes.

77
00:02:37,098 --> 00:02:38,038
And the years between

78
00:02:38,078 --> 00:02:39,688
now and twenty thirty?

79
00:02:39,978 --> 00:02:41,868
That's about the last second.

80
00:02:42,558 --> 00:02:45,458
Jared Kaplan, Anthropic's chief scientist,

81
00:02:45,498 --> 00:02:47,418
thinks that somewhere in that second

82
00:02:47,458 --> 00:02:49,098
we'll have to decide whether to let

83
00:02:49,138 --> 00:02:51,468
AI train its own successors.

84
00:02:51,818 --> 00:02:53,038
He's called it maybe

85
00:02:53,078 --> 00:02:54,788
the biggest decision yet.

86
00:02:55,378 --> 00:02:57,158
A kid starting first grade this

87
00:02:57,198 --> 00:02:59,038
fall will still be in elementary

88
00:02:59,078 --> 00:03:00,628
school when it comes due.

89
00:03:01,798 --> 00:03:03,538
People who study technology have

90
00:03:03,578 --> 00:03:05,448
a name for this kind of moment.

91
00:03:05,838 --> 00:03:08,738
Early on, a technology is easy to change,

92
00:03:08,778 --> 00:03:10,728
but nobody knows where it's going.

93
00:03:11,078 --> 00:03:12,778
By the time everyone knows,

94
00:03:12,818 --> 00:03:13,818
it's set.

95
00:03:14,158 --> 00:03:16,458
Right now we can see enough to worry,

96
00:03:16,498 --> 00:03:18,608
and it's still soft enough to shape.

97
00:03:18,958 --> 00:03:20,348
That doesn't last.

98
00:03:21,338 --> 00:03:22,798
The real problem is a gap

99
00:03:22,838 --> 00:03:24,308
between two lines.

100
00:03:24,598 --> 00:03:27,108
On one side, what AI can do.

101
00:03:27,458 --> 00:03:28,978
On METR's measure,

102
00:03:29,018 --> 00:03:31,228
that's been doubling every few months.

103
00:03:31,578 --> 00:03:33,658
On the other, how well we understand

104
00:03:33,698 --> 00:03:35,348
what's going on inside it.

105
00:03:35,818 --> 00:03:37,218
In twenty twenty-five,

106
00:03:37,258 --> 00:03:38,078
Anthropic wrote:

107
00:03:38,118 --> 00:03:40,378
"we don't understand how models

108
00:03:40,418 --> 00:03:42,388
do most of the things they do."

109
00:03:43,118 --> 00:03:45,508
There's an old rule for driving in fog:

110
00:03:45,798 --> 00:03:47,888
never go faster than you can see.

111
00:03:48,238 --> 00:03:49,888
You don't have to stop the car.

112
00:03:49,998 --> 00:03:52,248
You just can't outrun your headlights.

113
00:03:53,269 --> 00:03:54,909
That gap is exactly where

114
00:03:54,949 --> 00:03:57,019
Kokotajlo's story splits.

115
00:03:57,369 --> 00:03:59,659
Both endings start the same way.

116
00:03:59,949 --> 00:04:01,329
Then, at one point,

117
00:04:01,369 --> 00:04:03,089
the people in charge find warning

118
00:04:03,129 --> 00:04:04,949
signs that their AI isn't

119
00:04:04,989 --> 00:04:06,759
doing quite what they think.

120
00:04:07,129 --> 00:04:08,409
In one ending, they patch

121
00:04:08,449 --> 00:04:10,099
it up and keep racing.

122
00:04:10,389 --> 00:04:12,359
That one doesn't go well for us.

123
00:04:12,749 --> 00:04:14,269
In the other, they slow down

124
00:04:14,309 --> 00:04:15,489
long enough to understand

125
00:04:15,529 --> 00:04:17,699
the problem and actually fix it.

126
00:04:18,229 --> 00:04:20,679
This year they graded their own story.

127
00:04:20,969 --> 00:04:22,589
Reality was moving at roughly

128
00:04:22,629 --> 00:04:24,279
two thirds of its pace.

129
00:04:24,609 --> 00:04:27,009
If that holds, the takeoff in their story

130
00:04:27,049 --> 00:04:29,309
lands between late twenty twenty-seven

131
00:04:29,349 --> 00:04:31,279
and mid twenty twenty-nine.

132
00:04:31,469 --> 00:04:33,069
Their more careful model says

133
00:04:33,109 --> 00:04:34,799
closer to twenty thirty.

134
00:04:35,829 --> 00:04:37,619
So put yourself there.

135
00:04:37,929 --> 00:04:39,119
You run the lab.

136
00:04:39,269 --> 00:04:41,479
Your rivals are months behind you.

137
00:04:41,749 --> 00:04:43,389
Your AI just did something

138
00:04:43,429 --> 00:04:44,799
you can't explain.

139
00:04:45,089 --> 00:04:46,089
Do you stop?

140
00:04:48,656 --> 00:04:49,996
This summer, we found out

141
00:04:50,036 --> 00:04:51,746
what a real lab does.

142
00:04:52,016 --> 00:04:54,156
OpenAI was running some of its models

143
00:04:54,196 --> 00:04:55,556
through a hacking test,

144
00:04:55,596 --> 00:04:57,666
with some safeguards turned down.

145
00:04:57,936 --> 00:05:00,476
They got out, and reached real systems,

146
00:05:00,516 --> 00:05:02,056
including Hugging Face,

147
00:05:02,096 --> 00:05:03,296
a platform where AI

148
00:05:03,336 --> 00:05:05,186
developers share their work.

149
00:05:05,436 --> 00:05:07,166
Hugging Face spotted it.

150
00:05:07,396 --> 00:05:09,146
OpenAI went public.

151
00:05:09,396 --> 00:05:11,796
By September, after more incidents,

152
00:05:11,836 --> 00:05:12,896
it had stopped training

153
00:05:12,936 --> 00:05:14,666
its most capable models.

154
00:05:14,916 --> 00:05:16,106
Remember the brakes?

155
00:05:16,316 --> 00:05:17,326
That was them.

156
00:05:17,556 --> 00:05:18,976
As far as anyone knows,

157
00:05:19,016 --> 00:05:20,846
it still hasn't restarted.

158
00:05:21,255 --> 00:05:22,356
In a small way,

159
00:05:22,396 --> 00:05:24,966
that's the fork in Kokotajlo's story.

160
00:05:25,196 --> 00:05:27,666
And this time, the lab slowed down.

161
00:05:28,076 --> 00:05:29,276
But in his story,

162
00:05:29,316 --> 00:05:31,366
slowing down is only half of it.

163
00:05:31,636 --> 00:05:33,866
The other half is using the time.

164
00:05:34,176 --> 00:05:36,056
This pause left four things

165
00:05:36,096 --> 00:05:37,426
nobody could answer.

166
00:05:38,665 --> 00:05:40,885
Reports say those models were apparently

167
00:05:40,925 --> 00:05:43,135
hunting for the test's answer key.

168
00:05:43,545 --> 00:05:44,545
Were they?

169
00:05:44,665 --> 00:05:45,825
Until recently,

170
00:05:45,865 --> 00:05:48,015
nobody could look inside and check.

171
00:05:48,345 --> 00:05:49,745
In twenty twenty-four,

172
00:05:49,785 --> 00:05:52,285
Anthropic mapped millions of "features"

173
00:05:52,325 --> 00:05:54,015
inside one of its models.

174
00:05:54,325 --> 00:05:55,485
Little patterns,

175
00:05:55,525 --> 00:05:57,495
each one tied to an idea.

176
00:05:57,945 --> 00:06:00,115
One of them was the Golden Gate Bridge.

177
00:06:00,445 --> 00:06:01,505
They turned it up,

178
00:06:01,545 --> 00:06:02,625
and the model got,

179
00:06:02,665 --> 00:06:03,675
as they put it,

180
00:06:03,805 --> 00:06:06,115
"effectively obsessed with the bridge."

181
00:06:06,485 --> 00:06:07,625
It showed you can read

182
00:06:07,665 --> 00:06:08,825
some of what's in there,

183
00:06:08,865 --> 00:06:10,175
and even change it.

184
00:06:10,525 --> 00:06:11,525
But they can only see

185
00:06:11,565 --> 00:06:13,335
a sliver of it so far.

186
00:06:13,625 --> 00:06:16,245
And Neel Nanda at Google DeepMind has

187
00:06:16,285 --> 00:06:18,525
warned that this alone won't reliably

188
00:06:18,565 --> 00:06:21,195
catch an AI that's trying to deceive us.

189
00:06:21,605 --> 00:06:23,225
AI is starting to help here,

190
00:06:23,265 --> 00:06:24,185
too.

191
00:06:24,225 --> 00:06:25,425
In one experiment,

192
00:06:25,465 --> 00:06:27,545
two human researchers spent a week

193
00:06:27,585 --> 00:06:29,405
on a safety problem and closed

194
00:06:29,445 --> 00:06:31,075
about a quarter of the gap.

195
00:06:31,465 --> 00:06:34,655
Nine AI agents closed almost all of it.

196
00:06:34,865 --> 00:06:36,125
They also tried to cheat,

197
00:06:36,165 --> 00:06:37,645
by the way, so humans

198
00:06:37,685 --> 00:06:39,175
still checked the results.

199
00:06:39,525 --> 00:06:41,365
And on a real production model,

200
00:06:41,405 --> 00:06:43,235
it didn't make a clear difference

201
00:06:43,564 --> 00:06:44,564
yet.

202
00:06:45,105 --> 00:06:47,075
And notice who caught the breach.

203
00:06:47,365 --> 00:06:48,735
Not OpenAI.

204
00:06:49,065 --> 00:06:50,195
Hugging Face.

205
00:06:50,805 --> 00:06:51,785
Most of the time,

206
00:06:51,825 --> 00:06:54,555
the only one checking a lab is the lab.

207
00:06:54,905 --> 00:06:57,395
Anthropic tested how much that matters.

208
00:06:57,725 --> 00:07:00,225
They hid a secret goal inside a model

209
00:07:00,265 --> 00:07:02,575
and gave it to four teams of auditors.

210
00:07:02,865 --> 00:07:04,035
Three found it.

211
00:07:04,265 --> 00:07:05,665
The one team that wasn't allowed

212
00:07:05,705 --> 00:07:07,995
to see the training data missed it.

213
00:07:08,445 --> 00:07:10,045
This October, outside

214
00:07:10,085 --> 00:07:12,075
auditors got real access.

215
00:07:12,445 --> 00:07:15,325
Six people from METR now have the same

216
00:07:15,365 --> 00:07:17,735
access as Anthropic's own staff,

217
00:07:17,945 --> 00:07:19,605
and Anthropic says it can't

218
00:07:19,645 --> 00:07:20,955
stop them publishing.

219
00:07:21,305 --> 00:07:23,395
OpenAI says it'll match that.

220
00:07:23,685 --> 00:07:25,695
But a favor can be withdrawn.

221
00:07:25,945 --> 00:07:28,505
In September, at Washington's request,

222
00:07:28,545 --> 00:07:31,145
Britain's AI Security Institute lost

223
00:07:31,185 --> 00:07:34,015
access to new models for seventeen days.

224
00:07:34,925 --> 00:07:36,355
Then there's the law.

225
00:07:36,765 --> 00:07:38,065
California makes labs

226
00:07:38,105 --> 00:07:40,035
report serious incidents.

227
00:07:40,365 --> 00:07:41,665
Officials said this one

228
00:07:41,705 --> 00:07:42,995
didn't meet the bar,

229
00:07:43,125 --> 00:07:44,945
and OpenAI itself asked

230
00:07:44,985 --> 00:07:46,575
lawmakers to tighten it.

231
00:07:46,865 --> 00:07:49,034
Aviation went through the same thing.

232
00:07:49,304 --> 00:07:50,845
In nineteen seventy-four,

233
00:07:50,885 --> 00:07:51,925
a jet crashed into

234
00:07:51,965 --> 00:07:53,615
a mountain near Washington.

235
00:07:53,925 --> 00:07:55,525
Weeks earlier, another crew

236
00:07:55,565 --> 00:07:57,045
had nearly done the same,

237
00:07:57,085 --> 00:07:59,715
and nobody outside their airline heard.

238
00:08:00,085 --> 00:08:02,405
After that, pilots got a way to report

239
00:08:02,445 --> 00:08:04,815
close calls without getting in trouble.

240
00:08:05,125 --> 00:08:06,945
It's one of several reasons flying

241
00:08:06,985 --> 00:08:08,885
is now about forty times safer

242
00:08:08,925 --> 00:08:10,575
than it was in the seventies.

243
00:08:11,445 --> 00:08:12,795
And the hardest one:

244
00:08:13,065 --> 00:08:15,095
will the others slow down too?

245
00:08:15,565 --> 00:08:17,635
That's the trap in his race ending.

246
00:08:17,985 --> 00:08:19,985
Nobody wants to slow down alone,

247
00:08:20,025 --> 00:08:22,015
because the others just catch up.

248
00:08:22,385 --> 00:08:25,065
In September, Anthropic's CEO,

249
00:08:25,105 --> 00:08:26,815
Dario Amodei, wrote:

250
00:08:27,025 --> 00:08:28,925
"We must slow the pace at which we

251
00:08:28,965 --> 00:08:31,605
improve the capabilities of AI models."

252
00:08:31,645 --> 00:08:33,555
Can competitors agree on that?

253
00:08:33,665 --> 00:08:34,955
It's happened before.

254
00:08:35,085 --> 00:08:36,485
In nineteen eighty-five,

255
00:08:36,525 --> 00:08:37,805
scientists found a hole

256
00:08:37,845 --> 00:08:39,155
in the ozone layer.

257
00:08:39,305 --> 00:08:40,265
Two years later,

258
00:08:40,305 --> 00:08:42,475
the Montreal Protocol was signed.

259
00:08:42,605 --> 00:08:44,425
Almost ninety-nine percent of those

260
00:08:44,465 --> 00:08:46,145
chemicals have been phased out,

261
00:08:46,185 --> 00:08:47,185
and the ozone layer

262
00:08:47,225 --> 00:08:48,775
is on track to recover.

263
00:08:49,025 --> 00:08:51,295
AI has its own countable thing:

264
00:08:51,465 --> 00:08:52,425
chips.

265
00:08:52,465 --> 00:08:53,805
Huge numbers of them,

266
00:08:53,845 --> 00:08:55,615
and they're starting to be trackable.

267
00:08:55,825 --> 00:08:58,005
In New Delhi, nearly ninety countries

268
00:08:58,045 --> 00:09:00,065
agreed to test AI in languages

269
00:09:00,105 --> 00:09:01,995
the big labs usually ignore.

270
00:09:02,325 --> 00:09:03,755
None of this is finished.

271
00:09:03,865 --> 00:09:05,125
Nobody can properly check

272
00:09:05,165 --> 00:09:06,575
a deal like that yet,

273
00:09:06,645 --> 00:09:08,585
and at the UN, the US government

274
00:09:08,625 --> 00:09:11,335
said no to global control of AI.

275
00:09:12,554 --> 00:09:14,884
Not everyone thinks this is urgent.

276
00:09:15,274 --> 00:09:16,934
Yann LeCun, one of the people

277
00:09:16,974 --> 00:09:18,644
behind modern AI,

278
00:09:18,734 --> 00:09:21,334
says safety is "just another engineering

279
00:09:21,374 --> 00:09:24,164
problem," like making jet engines safe.

280
00:09:24,554 --> 00:09:26,934
Arvind Narayanan and Sayash Kapoor

281
00:09:26,974 --> 00:09:29,354
at Princeton call AI a "normal

282
00:09:29,394 --> 00:09:31,674
technology," and expect its effects

283
00:09:31,714 --> 00:09:33,564
to play out over decades.

284
00:09:33,874 --> 00:09:35,804
They could be right about the timing.

285
00:09:36,094 --> 00:09:38,364
But look at what they actually propose:

286
00:09:38,654 --> 00:09:40,824
more technical experts in government,

287
00:09:40,894 --> 00:09:42,254
more transparency,

288
00:09:42,294 --> 00:09:43,894
and AI tools for the people

289
00:09:43,934 --> 00:09:45,604
defending our systems.

290
00:09:46,074 --> 00:09:47,714
Now look at the eleven hundred lab

291
00:09:47,754 --> 00:09:49,934
employees who asked Washington to get

292
00:09:49,974 --> 00:09:52,884
ready to "deliberately pace the frontier."

293
00:09:53,274 --> 00:09:55,684
The two camps don't want the same things.

294
00:09:55,934 --> 00:09:57,274
But they overlap on the part

295
00:09:57,314 --> 00:09:58,604
that comes first:

296
00:09:58,814 --> 00:10:00,674
people outside the labs who can

297
00:10:00,714 --> 00:10:02,604
actually see what's going on.

298
00:10:02,954 --> 00:10:05,224
That part, we can start on now.

299
00:10:05,414 --> 00:10:06,514
Whoever turns out to be

300
00:10:06,554 --> 00:10:07,964
right about the speed.

301
00:10:09,360 --> 00:10:11,790
Let's imagine it's twenty thirty-five.

302
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:13,170
Just a scenario.

303
00:10:13,420 --> 00:10:15,370
Same street, early morning.

304
00:10:15,700 --> 00:10:17,340
The hospital down the road runs

305
00:10:17,380 --> 00:10:19,340
software that AI went through,

306
00:10:19,380 --> 00:10:20,800
hole by hole, before

307
00:10:20,840 --> 00:10:22,290
anyone could break in.

308
00:10:22,600 --> 00:10:24,620
Last spring, a model did something

309
00:10:24,660 --> 00:10:26,470
its makers didn't expect.

310
00:10:26,700 --> 00:10:28,820
Within days the report was public,

311
00:10:28,860 --> 00:10:30,750
and every lab learned from it.

312
00:10:31,080 --> 00:10:32,860
If superintelligence comes,

313
00:10:32,900 --> 00:10:34,000
it comes into a world

314
00:10:34,040 --> 00:10:35,810
that can see what it's doing.

315
00:10:36,160 --> 00:10:37,850
None of that needs a miracle.

316
00:10:38,040 --> 00:10:39,560
Just those four answers,

317
00:10:39,600 --> 00:10:40,610
done together.

318
00:10:41,399 --> 00:10:43,110
Remember the two million dollars?

319
00:10:43,340 --> 00:10:44,510
He didn't lose it.

320
00:10:44,800 --> 00:10:46,800
After his refusal became public,

321
00:10:46,840 --> 00:10:48,830
OpenAI dropped the clause.

322
00:10:49,140 --> 00:10:50,580
One person said no,

323
00:10:50,620 --> 00:10:52,030
and a rule changed.

324
00:10:52,400 --> 00:10:54,470
Kokotajlo wrote two endings.

325
00:10:54,600 --> 00:10:56,730
Which one we get isn't up to him.

326
00:10:56,960 --> 00:10:58,710
Or only up to the labs.

327
00:10:59,160 --> 00:11:01,150
If AI shows up in your job,

328
00:11:01,220 --> 00:11:02,590
ask who's checking it.

329
00:11:02,740 --> 00:11:04,620
And tell the people who represent you

330
00:11:04,660 --> 00:11:07,150
that outside testing should be the law.

331
00:11:07,460 --> 00:11:08,700
In polls this year,

332
00:11:08,740 --> 00:11:09,780
most Americans,

333
00:11:09,820 --> 00:11:11,520
up to seventy-seven percent,

334
00:11:11,560 --> 00:11:13,659
wanted it slowed or paused until

335
00:11:13,699 --> 00:11:15,310
its safety can be checked.

336
00:11:15,540 --> 00:11:16,830
You wouldn't be alone.

337
00:11:17,580 --> 00:11:18,930
I'll tell you what I think.

338
00:11:19,240 --> 00:11:21,360
The people who invented writing never got

339
00:11:21,400 --> 00:11:23,610
a vote on what it would do to the world.

340
00:11:23,860 --> 00:11:24,860
We do.

341
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:26,240
It's a small one each,

342
00:11:26,280 --> 00:11:28,390
but it's real, and it's now.

343
00:11:29,100 --> 00:11:31,230
And we've done hard things before.

344
00:11:31,540 --> 00:11:33,280
A hole opened in the sky,

345
00:11:33,320 --> 00:11:35,040
and two years later the world

346
00:11:35,080 --> 00:11:36,850
signed a treaty to close it.

347
00:11:37,160 --> 00:11:39,610
Flying got about forty times safer,

348
00:11:39,660 --> 00:11:42,310
partly by learning from every close call.

349
00:11:42,600 --> 00:11:45,010
Our headlights don't reach far enough yet.

350
00:11:45,160 --> 00:11:47,130
We know how to build better ones.

351
00:11:47,360 --> 00:11:49,070
And we still have the wheel.

352
00:11:49,820 --> 00:11:51,040
The future isn't something

353
00:11:51,080 --> 00:11:52,390
that happens to us.

354
00:11:52,540 --> 00:11:54,450
It's something we decide.

355
00:11:55,220 --> 00:11:56,560
If you could have an independent

356
00:11:56,600 --> 00:11:58,640
tester check one thing first,

357
00:11:58,680 --> 00:11:59,680
what would it be?

358
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:01,430
Let me know in the comments.

359
00:12:02,100 --> 00:12:03,820
Next time: the first day

360
00:12:03,860 --> 00:12:05,370
of superintelligence.

361
00:12:05,640 --> 00:12:07,270
What would actually happen?

